.venv
# The split CSVs are tracked deliberately (force-added) so the repository
# is retrainable. `data/*` blanket-ignores the folder so that a stray
# export or a scratch CSV cannot be committed by accident; everything
# that belongs in the repository is un-ignored by name below.
data/*
!data/README.md
!data/train.csv
!data/validation.csv
!data/test.csv
# The seven-day-ahead target, built from the three splits above by
# models/augment_future_score.py. Tracked so a clone can retrain the
# future-score model without regenerating it first, and so the exact
# target the shipped numbers were measured against is on the record.
!data/train_augmented.csv
!data/validation_augmented.csv
!data/test_augmented.csv
!data/archive_pre_user_split_fix/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
# Real secrets. .env carries the live JWT signing key; anything holding
# it could mint a valid session for any account on that deployment.
# .env.example is the tracked template and carries no values.
.env
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Real user data. Never commit this.
#
# storage/ is where the running app writes accounts (email + argon2
# password hash) and every user's logged history. It was tracked, and
# real accounts reached the public history of `main` as a result - see
# SECURITY.md, which also covers the part gitignoring cannot fix.
#
# The directory is kept (via .gitkeep) because the layout should be
# obvious in a fresh checkout; its contents never are. The app creates
# the files it needs on first write, so an empty directory is a
# complete, working starting state.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
storage/*
!storage/.gitkeep

# The same rule, unanchored. `storage/*` above contains a slash, so git
# anchors it to the repository root and it matches ONLY /storage/. That
# is not enough: services/storage/ is a PACKAGE, and for a while some
# modules resolved the project root one directory too deep and wrote
# their JSON into it. Six files of demo-account state were tracked
# before anyone noticed, because nothing about `git add -A` says so.
# These two patterns match a storage directory at any depth, so the
# next time something writes to the wrong place it stays untracked.
**/storage/*.json
**/storage/*.json.lock

# Lock/temp sidecars written next to the data during a transaction.
*.lock
*.tmp

# NOTE ON THE ARCHIVED MODELS.
#
# artifacts/archive_*/ holds the models as they were BEFORE the leakage
# fix and before the feature-consistency fix. Their .pkl files used to be
# ignored here as "20 MB nothing loads" - true of the running app, and
# the wrong call for a repository whose whole claim is that those two
# fixes were real. The metrics JSON says the numbers moved; only the
# fitted estimators let anyone check that for themselves.
#
# They are tracked. Nothing in the app loads them, and nothing should:
# they were fitted on a contaminated split and their feature lists do
# not match the current ones. They are evidence, not a fallback.
